Replacing $x$ by $\frac{x}{k}$ gives $y = f\left(\frac{x}{k}\right)$, a horizontal dilation from the $y$-axis that multiplies every $x$-coordinate by $k$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-4.0,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.1) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-3.6:3.2, samples=100, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.05*\x*\x*\x + 0.125*\x*\x - 0.6*\x - 0.6});
\draw[<-, red, thick] (-1.5,0.975) -- (-3.0,0.975);
\node[red] at (-2.25,1.5) {$\times k$};
\node[gray] at (-2.2,-1.6) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.7,-1.6) {$y=f(x/k)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Replacing $y$ by $\frac{y}{l}$ instead gives $y = l\,f(x)$, a vertical dilation from the $x$-axis that multiplies every $y$-coordinate by $l$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-2.4,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.8*\x*\x*\x + 1.0*\x*\x - 2.4*\x - 1.2});
\draw[->, red, thick] (0.667,-1.059) -- (0.667,-2.118);
\node[red, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0.667,-1.589) {$\times l$};
\node[gray] at (-1.2,-2.0) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.3,1.6) {$y=l\,f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
The hyperbola $y = \dfrac{1}{x - 2} + 4$ is dilated vertically from the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $3$.
State the equations of its asymptotes after this dilation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[->] (-3.5,0) -- (7.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,9.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,2,4,6} {\draw (\x,0.15) -- (\x,-0.15); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-1,2,4,6,8} {\draw (0.15,\y) -- (-0.15,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[dashed, thick] (2,-2.0) -- (2,9.0) node[above] {$x=2$};
\draw[dashed, thick] (-3.2,4) -- (7.2,4) node[right] {$y=4$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.0:1.82, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {1/(\x - 2) + 4});
\draw[thick, <->, domain=2.18:7.0, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {1/(\x - 2) + 4});
\node[above right] at (3,5) {$y = \dfrac{1}{x - 2} + 4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
The curve $y = x^2 - 4x + 3$ has $x$-intercepts at $x = 1$ and $x = 3$, and its vertex at $(2, -1)$.
Find the $x$-intercepts and vertex of the curve after a horizontal dilation by factor $3$ and a vertical dilation by factor $5$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (-1.5,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-0.4:4.4, samples=50, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x - 4*\x + 3});
\fill (1,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (3,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (2,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right] at (3.8,2.5) {$y = x^2 - 4x + 3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
